  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The four correct service combination options for AWS CodePipeline are:</p><ul><li><strong>Source: Bitbucket - Build: Jenkins - Deploy: Amazon S3</strong></li><li><strong>Source: S3 - Build: AWS CodeBuild - Deploy: AWS CodeDeploy</strong></li><li><strong>Source: CodeCommit - Build: AWS CodeBuild - Deploy: AWS CloudFormation</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Why these are correct:</strong><br>AWS CodePipeline is highly flexible and supports multiple third-party and AWS native integrations for each stage. These combinations reflect valid provider options in each stage:</p><ul><li><strong>Bitbucket/Jenkins/S3:</strong> CodePipeline supports Bitbucket as a source, Jenkins builds, and S3 as a deployment target for static asset pipelines.</li><li><strong>S3/CodeBuild/CodeDeploy:</strong> Amazon S3 can serve as a source, CodeBuild as the build platform, and CodeDeploy can be used for blue/green or in-place application deployment.</li><li><strong>CodeCommit/CodeBuild/CloudFormation:</strong> Using AWS services throughout ensures tight integration. CloudFormation can deploy infrastructure as code as the final stage.</li></ul><p><strong>Explanation of incorrect choices:</strong></p><ul><li><i>Source: Elastic Container Registry (ECR) - Build: Jenkins - Deploy: AWS OpsWorks</i>: While ECR is used to store container images, it is not a supported source in CodePipeline. Also, AWS OpsWorks is not natively supported as a deployment provider within CodePipeline.</li><li><i>Source: GitHub - Build: Amazon ECS - Deploy: AWS Elastic Beanstalk</i>: ECS is a container orchestration service, not a build tool. CodePipeline does not support ECS as a build provider.</li></ul><p><strong>Tips & Insights:</strong> When designing CodePipeline architectures, ensure the provider is natively supported at each stage. You can integrate with third-party tools such as Jenkins or GitHub as source or build providers, while AWS CodeBuild serves as a default managed build service. The deploy stage offers options such as S3, CodeDeploy, and CloudFormation but excludes services like OpsWorks or ECS as direct deploy or build providers.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Your organization has a history of using a mixture of AWS solutions, and up to now, most build pipelines are managed through Jenkins hosted on-premises. The upcoming quarter’s objective assigns your team the task of transferring these Jenkins-based pipelines into AWS CodePipeline. For this migration, all CodePipeline projects must be configured with three steps: source, build, and deploy. Which four of the following sets of service integrations can be correctly utilized in the construction of a new AWS CodePipeline? (Select Four.)</p>",
